1
00:00:00,230 --> 00:00:03,771
Imagine that the only thing
crossing a wire is the word hello.

2
00:00:04,416 --> 00:00:05,995
You can read it in an instant.

3
00:00:06,640 --> 00:00:08,560
So can someone listening to the wire.

4
00:00:09,729 --> 00:00:13,420
Inside the computer, each letter
is already a pattern of eight bits.

5
00:00:14,065 --> 00:00:15,494
Here is H in binary.

6
00:00:16,139 --> 00:00:19,680
We have changed the alphabet, but
the message is still plain to see.

7
00:00:20,849 --> 00:00:22,663
It gives us no secrecy at all.

8
00:00:23,308 --> 00:00:26,870
To hide the message, we need something
the listener cannot reproduce.

9
00:00:27,515 --> 00:00:30,438
Watch what happens when a
second stream meets the first.

10
00:00:31,083 --> 00:00:34,347
Five readable letters turn into
five quite different bytes.

11
00:00:35,516 --> 00:00:37,372
Where does that second stream come from?

12
00:00:38,017 --> 00:00:40,193
Follow one of its bits back along the wire.

13
00:00:40,838 --> 00:00:43,489
Inside the generator are
three rows of memory,

14
00:00:43,489 --> 00:00:45,809
a handful of connections, and a clock.

15
00:00:46,978 --> 00:00:48,045
This is Trivium.

16
00:00:48,690 --> 00:00:51,396
We'll trace one bit from its
birth inside the machine,

17
00:00:51,396 --> 00:00:53,831
all the way to the person
reading the message.

18
00:00:55,230 --> 00:00:58,921
Think of each bit of the secret
stream as an instruction for a switch.

19
00:00:59,489 --> 00:01:01,964
A zero says, leave the message bit alone.

20
00:01:02,532 --> 00:01:04,175
A one says, turn it over.

21
00:01:05,206 --> 00:01:07,104
The switch is called exclusive or.

22
00:01:07,673 --> 00:01:08,782
We'll write XOR.

23
00:01:09,351 --> 00:01:11,057
Zero with zero gives zero.

24
00:01:11,626 --> 00:01:13,845
With one input on, the result is one.

25
00:01:14,413 --> 00:01:17,528
With both inputs on, the
result drops back to zero.

26
00:01:18,558 --> 00:01:22,078
Now read a pair of streams from
left to right, one clock at a time.

27
00:01:22,647 --> 00:01:26,188
The third position is the first
place where our keystream says flip.

28
00:01:26,757 --> 00:01:29,104
Watch the output separate
from the message there.

29
00:01:30,134 --> 00:01:32,331
Send the result through
the same switch again.

30
00:01:32,900 --> 00:01:34,692
A bit that flipped once flips back.

31
00:01:35,261 --> 00:01:37,053
A bit that stayed still stays still.

32
00:01:37,621 --> 00:01:40,352
The receiver can now read
the original eight bits.

33
00:01:41,382 --> 00:01:45,670
Getting that identical sequence of
switches at both ends is the real trick.

34
00:01:46,239 --> 00:01:48,970
Let's open the generator
and see what it remembers.

35
00:01:50,230 --> 00:01:53,750
A register is a row of places
where the machine can hold bits.

36
00:01:54,270 --> 00:01:56,702
Here are the three rows
drawn to the same scale.

37
00:01:57,223 --> 00:02:00,145
The blue row has ninety-three
places, the teal row

38
00:02:00,145 --> 00:02:03,068
eighty-four, and the orange
row one hundred and eleven.

39
00:02:04,011 --> 00:02:06,528
Together they hold two
hundred and eighty-eight bits.

40
00:02:07,049 --> 00:02:09,267
Number the cells straight
through all three rows.

41
00:02:09,788 --> 00:02:11,366
Teal begins at ninety-four.

42
00:02:11,887 --> 00:02:14,490
Orange begins at one
hundred and seventy-eight.

43
00:02:15,433 --> 00:02:16,670
Look through this window.

44
00:02:17,190 --> 00:02:19,921
Filled cells hold one,
open cells hold zero.

45
00:02:20,441 --> 00:02:23,663
Enlarging a cell leaves its
address exactly where it was.

46
00:02:24,606 --> 00:02:28,296
When the clock ticks, a stored bit
moves one place along its own row.

47
00:02:28,817 --> 00:02:30,374
The bit at the far end leaves.

48
00:02:30,894 --> 00:02:34,030
That makes room at the entrance
for a freshly calculated bit.

49
00:02:34,974 --> 00:02:36,211
Trace the connections.

50
00:02:36,731 --> 00:02:40,955
Blue helps feed teal, teal helps feed
orange, and orange helps feed blue.

51
00:02:41,476 --> 00:02:44,057
To start this circuit we
need an initial pattern.

52
00:02:45,230 --> 00:02:47,918
Here is a public practice
key in hexadecimal.

53
00:02:48,108 --> 00:02:51,222
A real secret key stays with
the sender and receiver.

54
00:02:51,412 --> 00:02:54,057
Each pair of hex digits
represents one byte.

55
00:02:54,247 --> 00:02:56,124
Ten pairs give us eighty key bits.

56
00:02:56,468 --> 00:02:59,817
The other ten bytes are the
initialization vector, or I V.

57
00:03:00,007 --> 00:03:01,799
The receiver can know this value.

58
00:03:01,989 --> 00:03:04,958
Changing it lets the same
key start a different

59
00:03:04,958 --> 00:03:06,938
message with a different
internal arrangement.

60
00:03:07,282 --> 00:03:09,372
The key fills the first eighty blue cells.

61
00:03:09,562 --> 00:03:11,813
The vector fills positions
ninety-four through

62
00:03:11,813 --> 00:03:14,063
one hundred and seventy-three in teal.

63
00:03:14,253 --> 00:03:15,960
Notice which end enters first.

64
00:03:16,149 --> 00:03:18,613
In the reference byte
convention, the high bit of

65
00:03:18,613 --> 00:03:21,077
the last printed byte
goes into the first cell.

66
00:03:21,421 --> 00:03:25,666
Fill the thirteen spare blue places and
the four spare teal places with zero.

67
00:03:25,856 --> 00:03:28,224
Orange starts with one
hundred and eight zeros.

68
00:03:28,414 --> 00:03:31,201
Its last three positions,
two hundred and eighty-six

69
00:03:31,201 --> 00:03:33,640
through two hundred and
eighty-eight, hold ones.

70
00:03:33,984 --> 00:03:35,776
Every position now has a value.

71
00:03:35,966 --> 00:03:39,656
Before we let this arrangement evolve,
let's meet the rule that changes it.

72
00:03:40,230 --> 00:03:42,118
For a clock with interesting inputs,

73
00:03:42,118 --> 00:03:45,265
jump ahead to the first
output clock of our example.

74
00:03:45,509 --> 00:03:47,813
Freeze the state just
before the clock ticks.

75
00:03:48,057 --> 00:03:50,873
Every wire we follow reads
this same photograph.

76
00:03:51,315 --> 00:03:52,360
Begin in blue.

77
00:03:52,604 --> 00:03:55,612
A tap is simply a wire that
reads a particular cell.

78
00:03:55,856 --> 00:03:57,755
Read sixty-six and ninety-three.

79
00:03:57,999 --> 00:03:59,513
They contain one and zero.

80
00:03:59,757 --> 00:04:03,149
Their exclusive or gives the
first intermediate signal, one.

81
00:04:03,592 --> 00:04:08,008
In teal, read one hundred and sixty-two
and one hundred and seventy-seven.

82
00:04:08,251 --> 00:04:10,278
Zero and one give us one again.

83
00:04:10,522 --> 00:04:13,160
In orange, positions two
hundred and forty-three

84
00:04:13,160 --> 00:04:16,175
and two hundred and
eighty-eight both hold zero.

85
00:04:16,419 --> 00:04:17,763
Their signal is zero.

86
00:04:18,206 --> 00:04:20,040
Bring those three signals together.

87
00:04:20,284 --> 00:04:22,140
One exclusive or one is zero.

88
00:04:22,384 --> 00:04:25,542
Combining that zero with
the last zero leaves zero.

89
00:04:25,786 --> 00:04:27,450
That is our first keystream bit.

90
00:04:27,693 --> 00:04:30,595
The register entrances
still need their own inputs.

91
00:04:31,037 --> 00:04:34,152
Look at the neighboring blue
taps, ninety-one and ninety-two.

92
00:04:34,396 --> 00:04:35,889
This time the gate is AND.

93
00:04:36,133 --> 00:04:38,714
Both cells contain one, so the gate opens.

94
00:04:38,958 --> 00:04:42,265
If either input were zero,
its contribution would vanish.

95
00:04:42,509 --> 00:04:44,728
This product makes the feedback nonlinear.

96
00:04:45,170 --> 00:04:49,628
Combine that product with the blue signal
and the bit at one hundred and seventy-one.

97
00:04:49,872 --> 00:04:51,622
The first feedback value is one.

98
00:04:51,866 --> 00:04:53,359
Follow the other two paths.

99
00:04:53,603 --> 00:04:57,294
Each takes its own pair of neighbors
and one tap from a different row.

100
00:04:57,736 --> 00:05:01,298
All three feedback values happen
to be one in this photograph.

101
00:05:01,542 --> 00:05:03,100
Their destinations matter.

102
00:05:03,344 --> 00:05:07,120
The third goes to blue, the first
to teal, and the second to orange.

103
00:05:07,562 --> 00:05:08,821
Now release the clock.

104
00:05:09,065 --> 00:05:12,414
Three new bits enter together
as the old bits shift one place.

105
00:05:12,658 --> 00:05:17,116
Reading even one tap after moving
its row could change the calculation.

106
00:05:17,360 --> 00:05:19,558
The snapshot keeps that boundary precise.

107
00:05:20,230 --> 00:05:22,363
Rewind to the moment we loaded the key.

108
00:05:22,777 --> 00:05:25,103
Use exactly the clock rule we just traced.

109
00:05:25,517 --> 00:05:28,311
Here is the first tick, and
then the second and third.

110
00:05:28,725 --> 00:05:32,245
Red marks a position whose value
differs from the previous tick.

111
00:05:32,996 --> 00:05:35,322
Let the counter run to two
hundred and eighty-eight,

112
00:05:35,322 --> 00:05:37,391
the number of bits in the whole state.

113
00:05:37,805 --> 00:05:40,459
The rows have different
lengths, so this is a

114
00:05:40,459 --> 00:05:42,818
counting landmark, not a
return to the beginning.

115
00:05:43,568 --> 00:05:46,742
At five hundred and
seventy-six ticks, the key bits

116
00:05:46,742 --> 00:05:49,563
have repeatedly met bits
arriving from other rows.

117
00:05:49,977 --> 00:05:52,281
Every image here comes
from stepping the machine.

118
00:05:52,695 --> 00:05:55,980
As the counter accelerates we
sample its states for the screen.

119
00:05:56,731 --> 00:06:00,550
The standard warm-up ends after
eleven hundred and fifty-two clocks.

120
00:06:00,964 --> 00:06:03,929
Every candidate output so
far has been discarded.

121
00:06:04,343 --> 00:06:06,941
The very next clock
produces the first bit that

122
00:06:06,941 --> 00:06:09,250
may actually be used to encrypt a message.

123
00:06:10,230 --> 00:06:11,361
Return to hello.

124
00:06:11,468 --> 00:06:14,945
Both endpoints have the same
key and initialization vector.

125
00:06:15,053 --> 00:06:17,911
Independently, they arrive
at the same warmed state.

126
00:06:18,018 --> 00:06:19,576
One will send five bytes.

127
00:06:19,683 --> 00:06:22,094
The other needs forty
matching keystream bits.

128
00:06:22,288 --> 00:06:26,896
In ordinary hexadecimal notation the
first plaintext byte is forty-eight.

129
00:06:27,004 --> 00:06:29,585
The two L's have exactly
the same byte value.

130
00:06:29,692 --> 00:06:32,611
On this page, each binary
byte is written in the

131
00:06:32,611 --> 00:06:35,239
familiar way, with its
highest place on the left.

132
00:06:35,433 --> 00:06:37,545
The cipher fills the lowest place first.

133
00:06:37,653 --> 00:06:41,578
Follow the early clock bits into the
right-hand end of the first byte.

134
00:06:41,685 --> 00:06:45,248
After eight clocks the completed
mask byte reads F four.

135
00:06:45,355 --> 00:06:47,126
Four more bytes fill in beside it.

136
00:06:47,320 --> 00:06:49,603
Apply exclusive or down each column.

137
00:06:49,710 --> 00:06:51,481
The first byte becomes B C.

138
00:06:51,588 --> 00:06:53,231
Look at the two identical L's.

139
00:06:53,338 --> 00:06:57,349
They land under different keystream bytes
and give different ciphertext bytes.

140
00:06:57,543 --> 00:07:01,319
At the receiver, pair each ciphertext
byte with its original mask.

141
00:07:01,426 --> 00:07:04,925
The same gate brings back the
five letters, including both L's.

142
00:07:05,120 --> 00:07:07,732
With a fixed key, use
a fresh initialization

143
00:07:07,732 --> 00:07:09,365
vector for each separate message.

144
00:07:09,472 --> 00:07:12,583
Repeating the pair repeats the entire mask,

145
00:07:12,583 --> 00:07:14,806
exposing relationships
between the messages.

146
00:07:15,230 --> 00:07:17,193
Make a second copy of the experiment.

147
00:07:17,395 --> 00:07:21,214
Keep the key fixed, and flip just
the high bit of the last vector byte.

148
00:07:21,416 --> 00:07:25,469
In the loaded state that changes
exactly one cell, the first one in teal.

149
00:07:25,671 --> 00:07:27,037
Everything else agrees.

150
00:07:27,403 --> 00:07:28,961
Clock both machines together.

151
00:07:29,163 --> 00:07:32,597
A discrepancy can move along
a row before it reaches a tap.

152
00:07:32,800 --> 00:07:36,853
When it enters a feedback calculation,
it can influence another row as well.

153
00:07:37,055 --> 00:07:39,061
Watch the locations marked in red.

154
00:07:39,427 --> 00:07:41,432
How do we count the effect on the output?

155
00:07:41,635 --> 00:07:43,619
Write the two streams one above the other.

156
00:07:43,821 --> 00:07:46,893
Add one when their next bits
differ and zero when they agree.

157
00:07:47,095 --> 00:07:49,399
The running total is
called Hamming distance.

158
00:07:49,766 --> 00:07:53,520
Here the horizontal distance is
the number of output bits compared.

159
00:07:53,722 --> 00:07:55,962
Each upward step is one disagreement.

160
00:07:56,165 --> 00:07:59,237
After the full warm-up,
sixty-six of the first hundred

161
00:07:59,237 --> 00:08:01,967
and twenty-eight bits differ
for this particular pair.

162
00:08:02,334 --> 00:08:05,065
Restart both copies and
try shorter warm-ups.

163
00:08:05,267 --> 00:08:08,595
At two hundred and eighty-eight
clocks, fifty-one outputs differ.

164
00:08:08,797 --> 00:08:11,720
At five hundred and
seventy-six, sixty-three differ.

165
00:08:11,922 --> 00:08:14,183
These shorter runs are
teaching experiments.

166
00:08:14,385 --> 00:08:17,725
The specified cipher
uses all eleven hundred

167
00:08:17,725 --> 00:08:19,633
and fifty-two initialization clocks.

168
00:08:20,230 --> 00:08:22,861
Take four thousand and
ninety-six successive bits

169
00:08:22,861 --> 00:08:25,115
from the original, fully
initialized machine.

170
00:08:25,352 --> 00:08:27,016
What can we measure about this one sample?

171
00:08:27,252 --> 00:08:29,577
Start by sorting the
bits into zeros and ones.

172
00:08:30,006 --> 00:08:33,867
There are two thousand and forty-four
zeros and two thousand and fifty-two ones.

173
00:08:34,103 --> 00:08:36,122
The frequency test measures that imbalance

174
00:08:36,122 --> 00:08:38,477
against what independent
fair bits would produce.

175
00:08:38,905 --> 00:08:40,292
Now keep the original order.

176
00:08:40,528 --> 00:08:42,981
A run is a maximal
stretch of identical bits.

177
00:08:43,218 --> 00:08:44,839
Every change starts a new run.

178
00:08:45,076 --> 00:08:47,230
Our sample has two
thousand and fourteen runs.

179
00:08:47,466 --> 00:08:51,498
A perfectly alternating sequence could be
balanced and still have far too many runs.

180
00:08:51,927 --> 00:08:54,256
Each p-value measures
how unusual the statistic

181
00:08:54,256 --> 00:08:56,919
is under a model of independent fair bits.

182
00:08:57,155 --> 00:08:58,521
Both exceed one percent.

183
00:08:58,757 --> 00:09:00,848
This describes two
properties of one sample.

184
00:09:01,084 --> 00:09:03,793
Establishing security is
a much larger question.

185
00:09:04,222 --> 00:09:06,227
For correctness, use a published answer.

186
00:09:06,463 --> 00:09:09,307
Load the key and vector
from the eSTREAM record,

187
00:09:09,307 --> 00:09:11,519
generate sixty-four bytes,
and compare every byte.

188
00:09:11,756 --> 00:09:14,572
Expected and calculated
match all the way to the end.

189
00:09:15,230 --> 00:09:17,427
Now we can follow the entire journey.

190
00:09:17,614 --> 00:09:20,890
The secret key and public
initialization vector supply

191
00:09:20,890 --> 00:09:23,758
the starting bits for
three connected registers.

192
00:09:23,944 --> 00:09:26,802
The three ones at the end
complete the starting pattern.

193
00:09:27,140 --> 00:09:28,143
Turn the clock.

194
00:09:28,329 --> 00:09:31,371
Exclusive or combines taps,
AND introduces a product,

195
00:09:31,371 --> 00:09:34,793
and three feedback bits
cross into their next rows.

196
00:09:34,979 --> 00:09:39,865
Repeat the same rule eleven hundred and
fifty-two times before keeping any output.

197
00:09:40,202 --> 00:09:42,506
One more tick gives one keystream bit.

198
00:09:42,692 --> 00:09:44,954
A fresh message bit meets it at the switch.

199
00:09:45,140 --> 00:09:48,383
The receiver recreates that
switch and recovers the letter.

200
00:09:48,569 --> 00:09:51,129
We have followed the wire
all the way back to hello.

201
00:09:51,466 --> 00:09:55,050
Three registers, one clock,
and a small set of operations.

202
00:09:55,237 --> 00:09:56,687
That's the beauty of Trivium.

203
00:09:56,874 --> 00:09:58,239
Simple operations.

204
00:09:58,425 --> 00:09:59,662
Complex behavior.
